---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="tetragon"
    description="a full-stack tournament logistics platform for real-time operations."
>
    <a href="/" class="text-sm">return home </a>
    <header class="mb-4 mt-4">
        <h4 class="text-sm text-gray-500">2026</h4>
        <h1 class="mb-4">tetragon</h1>
        <p>
            a full-stack tournament logistics platform for real-time operations.
        </p>
        <div
            class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-2 my-4 font-mono text-sm text-gray-500 lowercase"
        >
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5">next.js</span
            >
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5"
                >typescript</span
            >
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5">react</span>
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5"
                >supabase</span
            >
            <span class="rounded border border-[#333] px-2 py-0.5"
                >tailwindcss</span
            >
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-4 text-sm">
            <a href="https://github.com/smst/tetragon" target="_blank">
                github ↗
            </a>
        </div>
    </header>

    <article>
        <hr />
        <h3>motivation</h3>
        <p>
            Every year, running a 200-person math and science tournament
            involves a chaotic web of Google Sheets, paper grading rubrics, and
            manual data entry. When you have dozens of volunteers trying to
            check in participants, grade individual and team rounds, and
            calculate live scoreboards simultaneously, standard spreadsheets
            buckle under the concurrency. Race conditions overwrite scores, and
            calculating final results takes hours. Tetragon was engineered to
            replace that fragile system with a unified, real-time logistics
            dashboard.
        </p>
        <hr />
        <h3>architecture</h3>
        <p>
            The platform is a Next.js application using the App Router, deployed
            on Vercel. The entire backend—Postgres database, authentication, and
            real-time state—is offloaded to Supabase.
        </p>
        <p>
            Instead of a monolithic interface, the UI is heavily decoupled into
            specialized modules based on the user's role. The dashboard routes
            volunteers to specific views, such as the staff management panel,
            proctor guide, or grading panel. When a proctor submits a score via
            the individual or team grading components, the payload hits a
            dedicated Next.js API route, which securely communicates with
            Supabase.
        </p>

        <pre><code>Client (Proctor) --POST /api/calculate-scores--> Next.js API (Vercel)
Next.js --> Supabase Postgres (Row-Level Security)
Supabase --Realtime Subscription--> Client (ScoreboardPanel)</code></pre>
        <hr />
        <h3>challenges</h3>
        <hr />
        <h3>outcome</h3>
        <hr />
        <div class="flex gap-4 text-sm">
            <a href="https://github.com/smst/tetragon" target="_blank">
                github ↗
            </a>
            <a href="/">return home </a>
        </div>
    </article>
</Layout>
